  "subtitle": "A Japanese word processor keyboard that moved the shift keys to the thumbs — so both hands stay on the home row and every key types three characters",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum has dozens of text-entry devices but nothing where the keyboard's whole design was driven by the anatomy of the thumb. The OASYS 100 thumb-shift keyboard put two extra keys under the thumbs so each regular key produced three different characters by which thumb shift was held — an anatomy-inspired input relocation that made fast Japanese kana-kanji composition possible. A commercially successful, culturally revered, and genuinely embodied interaction model with freely-licensed photos.",
